From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] DataExchange, Interface - guard Interface_Static's shared
 parameter table with a recursive mutex

Interface_Static's backing store (MoniTool_TypedValue::Stats(), a process-wide
NCollection_DataMap<TCollection_AsciiString, occ::handle<Standard_Transient>>
mapping parameter name to the boxed Interface_Static object holding its value)
and each individual named parameter's own mutable value fields (theival/thehval/
theoval, inherited from MoniTool_TypedValue, mutated in place by
SetCStringValue/SetIntegerValue/SetRealValue/SetUptodate and read by
CStringValue/IntegerValue/RealValue/UpdatedStatus) have no synchronization of
their own. Every STEP/IGES read/write call (STEPControl_Reader/Writer,
STEPCAFControl_Reader/Writer, IGESControl_Reader/Writer, IGESCAFControl_Reader/
Writer) reads and writes this table repeatedly throughout the operation, not
just at entry: BRepToIGES_BREntity, GeomToIGES_GeomCurve/GeomSurface,
IGESToBRep_Actor/CurveAndSurface, StepToGeom and XSAlgo_ShapeProcessor all call
Interface_Static::IVal/RVal/CVal once per entity/curve/surface translated, and
XSControl_Controller::Customise (run on every STEPControl_Reader/Writer and
IGESControl_Reader/Writer construction, via SelectNorm -> SetController ->
Customise) calls Interface_Static::Items(), which iterates the whole map, on
every single reader/writer construction regardless of whether the caller ever
calls SetCVal/SetIVal/SetRVal itself.

SetCStringValue in particular mutates a shared, reference-counted
TCollection_HAsciiString buffer in two separate steps
(thehval->Clear(); thehval->AssignCat(val);), so two threads concurrently
setting the same parameter race on the SAME string buffer object, not merely on
independent copies: a genuine memory-safety hazard (buffer corruption), not
only a logical one.

Reachable from every OCCT consumer of Interface_Static, not just OCCTSwift's
own bridge (which already mitigates this bridge-side via igesMutex(), see
OCCTSwift#181-B/#359). Confirmed via OCCTSwift#1157's own TSan reproducer
(Scripts/repro/1157-interface-static-thread-safety/occt_1157_stress.cpp): the
same STEPControl_Writer/Reader and IGESControl_Writer/Reader sequences the
bridge uses, run concurrently on independent shapes/files with no lock, race on
MoniTool_TypedValue::Stats()'s NCollection_DataMap under ThreadSanitizer.

Fixed by taking a single, function-local static, thread-safe-lazy-init (C++11
magic statics) std::recursive_mutex for the whole body of every
Interface_Static static entry point that touches Stats() or a per-parameter
value: Init (both overloads), Static, IsPresent, CDef, IDef, IsSet, CVal, IVal,
RVal, SetCVal, SetIVal, SetRVal, Update, IsUpdated, Items, FillMap.
SetUptodate()/UpdatedStatus() (Interface_Static's own instance methods) need no
separate lock, since their only two call sites (Update/IsUpdated) already hold
this one before calling them, and Items() (the third caller of
UpdatedStatus()) does too. Recursive, not a plain mutex, because several of
these methods call back into others declared here on the same thread: Init's
Interface_ParamMisc branch calls Static(), the '&' edit-syntax branch calls
Static() then mutates the returned handle's own fields directly (SetIntegerLimit/
SetRealLimit/SetUnitDef/StartEnum/AddEnum, all further MoniTool_TypedValue
mutation, out of scope for THIS defect since none of those touch
theival/thehval/theoval or Stats() beyond the one Static() lookup already
covered), and Init(char) calls the Interface_ParamType overload then Static()
again for the 'p' post-check. A plain mutex would deadlock the very first
Interface_Static::Init call for the "step"/"XSTEP" family
(STEPControl_Controller's constructor makes ~50 such calls under its own
mutex-guarded once-only registration, several of them the Interface_ParamMisc
form), the same self-deadlock shape OCCTSwift#1153's rejected first attempt hit
for a different class (see this project's own CLAUDE.md "Known OCCT Bugs"
entry for #1153).

thelibtv (MoniTool_TypedValue.cxx's OTHER file-scope NCollection_DataMap,
backing AddLib/Lib/FromLib/LibList/StaticValue) has the identical shape and is
NOT fixed here: grepped the whole DataExchange module for its three writers/
readers and found no call reachable from STEPControl/IGESControl/XSAlgo's
STEP/IGES read/write call chains (its only two callers in the tree are
Interface_GTool.cxx, an unrelated generic-tool registration mechanism, and
MoniTool_TypedValue.cxx's own methods), so it is out of scope for the
OCCTSwift#1157 investigation this patch closes; a real, same-shape,
unaddressed sibling for a future pass, same as OCCTSwift#1371's own "not
fixed: GLOBAL_faces2d" note for a different toolkit.

This is a partial fix, deliberately: it makes Interface_Static's shared
storage memory-safe under concurrent access, but does NOT make two
independent, concurrently-running STEP/IGES operations that set DIFFERENT
values for the SAME named parameter (e.g. two threads writing STEP with
different write.step.schema settings at once) produce correct, uncorrupted-by-
each-other output. Interface_Static functions as a shared global used as an
IMPLICIT parameter-passing channel between a caller's SetCVal/SetIVal/SetRVal
and reads many stack frames below it inside Transfer()/Write()/ReadFile(), and
no amount of locking the accessor calls themselves closes the window between
one thread's Set and its own later (same-thread) Get. OCCTSwift#1157 measured
this empirically: a variant of this exact per-accessor lock, wrapped around a
scenario that sets then reads the same parameter with a yield() in between,
still cross-talks between two concurrent threads 100% of the time, the
identical rate as with no lock at all. OCCTSwift's own igesMutex() therefore
stays in place after this patch, unchanged: it serializes the whole
configure-then-run window per operation, which this mutex deliberately does
not attempt.

`thread_local` storage, the fix this project's own #1157 investigation
started from, was considered and rejected: STEPControl_Controller's
constructor, IGESControl_Controller's constructor, and IGESData::Init() each
gate their parameter registration (~50 Interface_Static::Init calls apiece)
behind a process-global one-time-only guard (a function-local static bool, or
Interface_InterfaceModel::HasTemplate("iges")). Making the backing map
thread_local would let only the first thread ever to touch STEP/IGES in the
process populate its own copy; every other thread's thread-local copy would
stay permanently empty (the registration guard, itself not thread_local, would
report "already done" and skip re-running), so Interface_Static::CVal/IVal/RVal
would silently return ""/0 for every parameter on any thread but the first,
worse than the current race and invisible to any reproducer that runs one
operation per thread.
---
